180	60	11	\N	To alleviate nasal congestion, it's essential to manage symptoms while maintaining good hygiene and taking appropriate measures if necessary. Here are some non-pharmacological methods you might consider based on general medical advice that is commonly understood but should not replace professional healthcare recommendations:\n\n1. Stay hydrled - Drinking plenty of fluids can thin mucus, making it easier to expel and potentially reducing nasal congestion. Warm liquids like herbal teas may also offer some symptomatic relief due to their soothing effect on the throat.\n\n2. Use a humidifier - Moist air from room diffusers or cool mist humidifiers can help moisten dry nasal passages, making breathing easier and helping reduce congestion. Be sure to clean your device regularly as it's essential in preventing bacteria growth that could cause other issues like mold or mildew buildup.\n\n3. Nasal saline irrigation - Regularly using a sterile nasal rinse, such as Neti Pots with saltwater solution (saline), can help clear out the congested nasal passages effectively without harmful side effects when done correctly and safely under guidance from healthcare professionals.\n\n4. Rest – Ensure you get plenty of sleep to aid your body's immune response in fighting off any potential viral or bacterial causes for congestion, which may be influencing its severity. \n\n5. Proper nutrition - Eat a balanced diet rich in fruits and vegetables can support overall health and potentially reduce the impact of symptoms like nasal congestion due to their high content of vitamins C & D, zinc, and other immune-boosting compounds that may help.\n\n6. Steamy showers – Inhaling steam during a warm or hot bath can soothe irritated tissues in the nose and throat associated with congestion due to warming up mucus secretions making them easier to expectorate out of your nasal passages. \n\n7. Over-the-counter remedies - Nasal decongestant sprays or drops can offer temporary relief from acute symptoms, but should not be used for more than three days in a row due to potential rebound congestion (rhinitis medicamentosa). Oral antihistamines might also provide some benefits if you suspect allergic rhinitis is contributing.\n\n8. Saline sprays - These help moisten the nasal passages and relieve irritation, but like any intervention involving your nose or respiratory system should be used with guidance from healthcare professionals to avoid infection risk due to potential improper use of homemade solutions.\n\n9. Avoid known allergens - If congestion is allergy-related, identify and minimize exposure to common triggers like dust mites, pollen, mold, pet dander or certain foods that can exacerbate symptoms as much as possible for the time being while avoiding excessive strain on your immune system.\n\nRemember: These suggestions serve only a general purpose and are not tailored to any specific health conditions you might have underlying reasons leading them, like chronic rhinosinusitis or nasal polyps requiring different treatments such as antibiotics prescribed by doctors based on cultures when necessary, corticosteroids inhalers for inflammatory diseases of the nose and sinuses, etc. Always consult a healthcare professional before starting any new treatment to ensure safety and efficacy tailored specifically towards your unique situation.	f	\N
181	61	8	\N	As a medical expert, I can confidently say that both lutein and omega-3 fatty acids have been studied extensively for their potential benefits on visual health.\n\nLutein is a carotenoid pigment found in high concentrations in the macula, the part of the retina responsible for central vision. Research has shown that lutein supplementation:\n\n1. May reduce the risk of age-related macular degeneration (AMD) by 40-50% (AREDS2 study)\n2. Can improve visual acuity and contrast sensitivity in individuals with AMD (AREDS2 study)\n3. Has antioxidant properties, which may help protect the eyes from oxidative stress and inflammation\n\nOmega-3 fatty acids, particularly docosahexaenoic acid (DHA), are also important for eye health. DHA is a primary structural component of the retina and is involved in photoreceptor function.\n\nStudies have shown that omega-3 supplements:\n\n1. May improve visual acuity and reduce the risk of AMD progression (Age-Related Eye Disease Study 2, AREDS2)\n2. Can enhance contrast sensitivity and color vision\n3. Have anti-inflammatory properties, which may help protect the eyes from oxidative stress and inflammation\n\nCombining lutein and omega-3 supplements has been shown to have synergistic effects on visual health. A study published in the Journal of Ophthalmic Research found that combining 10 mg/day of lutein with 500 mg/day of omega-3 fatty acids improved visual acuity and reduced oxidative stress in patients with AMD.\n\nIn summary, both lutein and omega-3 fatty acids have been shown to be beneficial for vision health, and combining them may provide additional benefits. However, it's essential to note that while these supplements show promise, they should not replace established treatment regimens for eye diseases. Consult with a healthcare professional before adding any supplements to your regimen.	f	\N
