205	71	6	\N	Right lower abdominal (RLQ) pain in a 33-year-old woman has several possible causes, ranging from minor to surgical emergencies. Key possibilities include:\n\n**Gynecologic (most urgent to rule out)**\n- **Ectopic pregnancy**: Life-threatening if ruptured; must rule out if there’s any chance of pregnancy (even with contraception)\n- **Ovarian torsion**: Sudden severe pain, nausea/vomiting; emergency requiring surgery to save the ovary\n- **Ruptured ovarian cyst**: Sudden sharp pain, often mid-cycle or after activity\n- **Pelvic inflammatory disease (PID)**: Pain with fever, abnormal discharge, painful intercourse\n- **Mittelschmerz**: Pain during ovulation (mid-cycle), usually milder\n\n**Gastrointestinal**\n- **Appendicitis**: Classically starts near belly button, moves to RLQ; worsens with movement/coughing; loss of appetite, low-grade fever, nausea\n- **Crohn’s disease/terminal ileitis**: Chronic diarrhea, weight loss, recurrent pain\n- **Cecal diverticulitis**: Similar to appendicitis but often less severe\n\n**Urinary**\n- **Kidney stone (ureter)**: Severe colicky pain radiating to groin, blood in urine, restlessness\n- **UTI**: Burning urination, frequency, suprapubic pain\n\n**Other**\n- Hernia, musculoskeletal strain, endometriosis\n\n**Go to the ER now if you have:**\n- Any chance of pregnancy (especially with dizziness, shoulder pain, or fainting)\n- Severe/worsening pain, rigid abdomen, or pain with rebound/guarding\n- Persistent vomiting or inability to keep fluids down\n- High fever or chills\n- Blood in stool or urine\n\n**Next steps:**\nIf symptoms are mild and none of the red flags apply, seek prompt evaluation (urgent care or primary care within 24–48 hours). A pregnancy test is essential if sexually active. Imaging (ultrasound or CT) and blood/urine tests are typically needed to confirm the cause.\n\n**To help narrow this down:** When did the pain start? Is it constant or cramping? Any fever, nausea, vomiting, urinary symptoms, or vaginal bleeding/discharge? When was your last menstrual period, and is pregnancy possible?	f	\N
206	72	6	\N	Elevated ALT itself does **not** cause diarrhea. ALT is simply a marker of liver cell inflammation or damage; it is not a substance that produces gastrointestinal symptoms.\n\nHowever, the underlying cause of your elevated ALT could potentially explain both findings:\n\n**Conditions that can raise ALT and cause diarrhea:**\n- **Viral hepatitis** (hepatitis A, B, E): Often causes nausea, abdominal pain, and sometimes diarrhea, especially in the acute phase\n- **Medication or supplement toxicity**: Many drugs that injure the liver (e.g., antibiotics, anti-seizure medications, herbal supplements) also cause diarrhea as a side effect\n- **Alcohol-related liver stress**: Excess alcohol intake commonly causes diarrhea\n- **Bile duct obstruction or cholestasis**: Reduced bile flow can lead to fatty stools (steatorrhea) — pale, foul-smelling diarrhea due to fat malabsorption\n- **Acute infectious hepatitis** from various pathogens\n\n**Unrelated coincidence:**\nOften, elevated ALT and diarrhea are unrelated. Common causes of transient diarrhea (viral gastroenteritis, foodborne illness, IBS, lactose intolerance) do not typically affect liver enzymes.\n\n**When to seek care:**\nIf you have persistent diarrhea (more than 2–3 days), dark urine, pale stools, jaundice (yellowing skin/eyes), severe abdominal pain, or significant ALT elevation (more than 2–3 times the upper limit of normal), you should see a clinician promptly to evaluate for hepatitis, bile duct issues, or other causes.\n\nFor an isolated mildly elevated ALT in an otherwise healthy adult, common culprits include recent strenuous exercise, alcohol use, fatty liver, or medication effects — but this should still be confirmed and monitored by your healthcare provider.	f	\N
