218	84	6	\N	Not necessarily—Metrogel (metronidazole vaginal gel) doesn't work for everyone, even when the diagnosis is correct.\n\n**Why it might fail despite being BV:**\n- **Biofilms:** The bacteria causing BV (mainly *Gardnerella*) can form protective biofilms on the vaginal wall, shielding them from antibiotics. This is a common cause of recurrent or persistent BV.\n- **Resistance:** While less common with metronidazole than other antibiotics, resistant strains do occur.\n- **Incomplete treatment:** Missing doses, stopping early, or having menstrual bleeding wash out the gel can reduce efficacy.\n- **Reinfection:** New or untreated sexual partners can reintroduce bacteria (though BV isn't strictly an STI, semen and new partners can disrupt vaginal pH).\n- **Co-infections:** BV often coexists with other infections (like trichomoniasis or yeast) that metronidazole doesn't treat.\n\n**Also consider:**\nIf Metrogel didn't help, the diagnosis could be wrong. Symptoms similar to BV can be caused by:\n- Trichomoniasis (requires different medication)\n- Yeast infection\n- Cytolytic vaginosis\n- Atrophic vaginitis (if menopausal)\n- Desquamative inflammatory vaginitis\n\n**Next steps:**\n- Return for a repeat wet mount/NAAT testing to confirm the diagnosis\n- If confirmed BV: Consider oral metronidazole, tinidazole, or clindamycin (oral or vaginal)\n- For recurrent cases: Extended pulse-dose therapy or boric acid protocols may be needed\n- Consider treating male partners in recurrent cases (evidence is mixed, but sometimes helpful)\n\n**Bottom line:** Treatment failure happens in roughly 15–30% of BV cases initially, so not responding to Metrogel doesn't rule out BV—but it does warrant follow-up testing to confirm what you're actually treating.\n\n*This is general information; please consult your healthcare provider for personalized evaluation.*	f	\N
