239	71	14	\N	Right lower abdominal pain in a 33-year‑old woman can come from many causes. I can’t diagnose over text, but here are the common possibilities, how to tell them apart, what to do now, and when to seek urgent care.\n\nCommon causes\n- Appendicitis — often starts as dull periumbilical pain that moves to the right lower quadrant, with nausea, fever, loss of appetite, and localized tenderness (rebound/guarding).\n- Ovarian torsion — sudden, severe unilateral pelvic pain with nausea/vomiting; ovary may be enlarged on ultrasound. Surgical emergency.\n- Ruptured ovarian cyst — sudden sharp pain, sometimes light vaginal bleeding; can cause internal bleeding.\n- Ectopic pregnancy — consider in any woman of childbearing potential with abdominal pain and/or vaginal bleeding; may have shoulder pain, dizziness if bleeding.\n- Pelvic inflammatory disease (PID) / tubo‑ovarian abscess — usually bilateral pelvic pain, fever, abnormal discharge, recent STI risk.\n- Urinary causes — UTI or ureteral (kidney) stone: dysuria, frequency, or colicky flank-to‑groin pain and hematuria.\n- Gastrointestinal — gastroenteritis, constipation, inflammatory bowel disease, or (less commonly at this age) cecal diverticulitis or mesenteric adenitis.\n- Gynecologic cyclic pain — mittelschmerz (ovulation pain) or endometriosis (chronic cyclical pain).\n\nKey clues from history/exam that help\n- Pregnancy test positive → prioritize ectopic pregnancy and obstetric/gynecologic causes.\n- Sudden severe onset with vomiting → think torsion or rupture or renal colic.\n- Fever and foul discharge → consider PID or abscess.\n- Pain migration from umbilicus to RLQ, tenderness at McBurney point, rebound → appendicitis.\n- Blood in urine → stone or UTI.\n- Recent intercourse or STI risk → PID or ectopic.\n\nWhat to do now\n- If you could be pregnant, do a pregnancy test immediately.\n- Seek urgent evaluation (emergency department or same‑day clinic) if you have any of: severe or worsening pain, fever, fainting/dizziness, fainting, heavy vaginal bleeding, progressive vomiting, inability to pass stool/gas, or signs of peritonitis (rigid abdomen).\n- If pain is mild and stable, make a prompt appointment with your primary care or gynecology clinic. Typical initial tests include: urine pregnancy test, urine dip/culture, CBC, CRP, pelvic exam, STI testing, and imaging (pelvic/transvaginal ultrasound is first-line for gynecologic causes; CT abdomen/pelvis often used for suspected appendicitis/other abdominal causes if not pregnant).\n\nIf you’d like, tell me:\n- Exact location, how it started and how severe it is,\n- Associated symptoms (fever, nausea, vomiting, vaginal bleeding/discharge, urinary symptoms, bowel changes),\n- Last menstrual period and contraceptive/sexual history,\n- Any previous similar episodes or abdominal surgery.\nWith that I can help prioritize next steps.	f	\N
240	72	14	\N	Short answer: No — an isolated rise in ALT itself does not cause diarrhea. ALT is a blood marker that signals liver cell injury; it’s a lab finding, not a symptom-causing toxin.\n\nWhy you might see both elevated ALT and diarrhea\n- They can occur together if the same underlying condition causes both. Examples:\n  - Viral hepatitis or some systemic infections can cause GI symptoms (nausea, abdominal pain, sometimes diarrhea).\n  - Some medications or antibiotics can raise ALT and also cause diarrhea as a side effect.\n  - Advanced or cholestatic liver disease can cause fat malabsorption or pale/greasy stools (steatorrhea), which may be perceived as diarrhea.\n  - Metabolic, autoimmune, or infiltrative diseases can involve the liver and gut in different ways.\n- They can also be unrelated problems that happen at the same time.\n\nWhat to do next\n- If you feel otherwise well and the ALT is only mildly elevated, common steps include repeating the test in 6–12 weeks and reviewing risk factors (alcohol, medications/supplements, obesity, viral hepatitis exposure).\n- A clinician will usually check a liver panel (AST, ALP, GGT, bilirubin), viral hepatitis serologies, metabolic/iron studies and may order an ultrasound or other tests depending on risk factors and results.\n- For diarrhea, evaluate timing, character (bloody, greasy, watery), duration, fever, recent antibiotics, travel, or foods. Stool studies or further GI workup may be needed if it is severe or persistent.\n\nWhen to seek urgent care\n- Jaundice (yellowing of skin/eyes), very dark urine, pale stools, severe abdominal pain, high fever, heavy bleeding, fainting, or rapid weight loss — these require prompt medical attention.\n\nIf you’d like, tell me your ALT value, how long it’s been elevated, any medications or recent illnesses, and details about the diarrhea (onset, appearance, frequency), and I can give more tailored guidance.	f	\N
