244	76	14	\N	You’re right — hydroxyzine doesn’t cure poison ivy. Here’s what it does and what usually helps:\n\n- What hydroxyzine does: it’s a first‑generation antihistamine (sedating). It can reduce itching and help you sleep, mainly by its sedating and central effects. Because poison‑ivy rash is a T‑cell–mediated allergic contact dermatitis (not purely histamine‑driven), antihistamines often give only partial relief of itch.  \n- What it does not do: it won’t remove the urushiol (the plant oil that causes the reaction), stop the immune process, or speed healing of the rash itself.\n- Typical treatments that do treat the rash or symptoms:\n  - Remove urushiol quickly after exposure by washing skin and clothing with soap and water (ideally within minutes).  \n  - Topical therapies for mild/localized rash: calamine lotion, cool compresses, oatmeal baths, and low‑potency topical hydrocortisone (OTC 1%) for itch.  \n  - Topical high‑potency corticosteroids or a prescription oral corticosteroid (e.g., prednisone) are often used for more widespread or severe reactions, or when the face/genitals/eyes are involved. (Short, low‑dose steroid courses can lead to rebound; doctors often prescribe an adequate dose and taper.)\n  - Antihistamines like hydroxyzine, cetirizine or loratadine can help relieve itching; hydroxyzine is useful at night because it sedates.  \n- Cautions: hydroxyzine can cause drowsiness, dry mouth and other anticholinergic effects; avoid mixing with other sedatives or drinking alcohol. Don’t use topical antihistamine creams (like diphenhydramine cream) long‑term — they can cause contact dermatitis.\n- See a clinician urgently if: the rash involves eyes/mouth/genitals, you have trouble breathing or swallowing, extensive body surface is affected, severe swelling (especially of the face), or signs of infection (increasing pain, pus, fever).\n\nIf you want, tell me how severe the rash is or what treatments you’ve already tried and I can suggest next steps.	f