249	81	14	\N	Not necessarily. Lisinopril begins to lower blood pressure within about an hour and peaks in effect after 6–8 hours, but the full, steady blood‑pressure lowering effect usually takes a few weeks (often 2–4 weeks, sometimes up to 6) after starting or changing the dose.\n\nWhat that means for you\n- You may see some fall in BP the first day, but don’t expect final/target control yet.  \n- Dose adjustments (if needed) are usually made after observing readings over days–weeks.\n\nWhat to do now\n- Measure and record your BP at home (sitting, after 5 minutes rest), for example morning and evening for the first week, and bring the log to your follow‑up.  \n- Continue the medicine as prescribed unless instructed otherwise.\n\nWhen to contact your clinician or get urgent care\n- Very high readings (e.g., systolic ≥180 or diastolic ≥120) or symptoms like chest pain, shortness of breath, fainting, severe dizziness.  \n- Signs of angioedema (swelling of face, lips, tongue or throat) — go to emergency care immediately.  \n- Severe lightheadedness or fainting (could be “first‑dose” hypotension, especially if you’re also on a diuretic).\n\nMonitoring and side effects\n- Common: dizziness, lightheadedness, dry cough.  \n- Important to check kidney function and potassium within 1–2 weeks after starting (especially if you have kidney disease, diabetes, or take diuretics/NSAIDs).  \n- Don’t stop or change the dose without talking to your prescriber.\n\nIf you’d like, tell me your current BP readings, other medications you take, and any symptoms — I can help interpret them and advise next steps.	f	\N
250	82	14	\N	Short answer: low‑strength hydrocortisone (OTC 0.5–1%) can be used on the face for short periods, but you should be careful. The facial skin is thin and more prone to steroid side effects, so stronger or prolonged use is not recommended.\n\nWhat can happen with facial use\n- Skin thinning (atrophy), easy bruising and visible blood vessels (telangiectasia)  \n- Steroid acne or worsening acneiform eruptions  \n- Perioral dermatitis or “steroid rosacea” with long or repeated use  \n- Delayed healing and increased risk of local infections  \n- If used near the eyelids for prolonged periods: increased risk of glaucoma or cataracts\n\nWhen it’s reasonable to use it\n- A short course (a few days to up to about 1–2 weeks) of low‑potency hydrocortisone for an itchy inflammatory flare (e.g., an insect bite, mild contact dermatitis) is commonly safe for adults.  \n- Use the smallest amount that helps, once or twice daily, and stop if it isn’t improving or if it worsens.\n\nWhen to avoid or see a clinician first\n- Don’t use higher‑potency topical steroids on the face.  \n- Avoid routine or long‑term use on the face, and avoid use in infants unless directed by a doctor.  \n- See a clinician if the problem is near the eyes, involves a large area, is recurrent, looks infected (pus, spreading redness, fever), or hasn’t improved within about 1–2 weeks.\n\nSafer alternatives for facial itch/inflammation\n- Gentle emollients/moisturizers and avoiding the trigger (fragrance, harsh soaps)  \n- Prescription non‑steroidal options for sensitive sites: topical calcineurin inhibitors (tacrolimus, pimecrolimus) — useful long‑term on face/eyelids  \n- Oral antihistamine for itch relief (sedating/non‑sedating as appropriate)  \n- For insect bites: cold compress, calamine, short topical hydrocortisone if needed\n\nIf you want, tell me what you’re trying to treat (rash, acne, itch, insect bite, etc.) and I can give more specific advice.	f	\N
251	83	14	\N	Trazodone is usually taken once daily, but the best time depends on why you're taking it and how it affects you.\n\nIf you're taking trazodone for sleep: Take it 30 minutes to 1 hour before bedtime. Trazodone is sedating for many people, so nighttime is ideal.\n\nIf you're taking trazodone for depression or anxiety: It’s often still taken at night, because it can make you drowsy, may cause dizziness when standing, and reduces next-day grogginess if taken before sleep.\n\nHowever, some people split the dose (morning + night) for higher doses—but only if prescribed.\n\nImportant notes: Never change the timing without your prescriber’s guidance. If it makes you too sleepy in the morning, taking it earlier in the evening can help. Avoid alcohol — it increases sedation. If you accidentally took it during the day, expect sleepiness and avoid driving.\n\nIf you tell me why you’re taking it (sleep or mood), your dose, and how it affects you, I can help you figure out the most ideal timing."	f	\N
252	84	14	\N	Not necessarily — Metrogel (topical metronidazole) often treats bacterial vaginosis (BV), but there are several reasons it might not work:\n\n- Wrong diagnosis: yeast infections, trichomonas, allergic/contact dermatitis or other causes can cause similar symptoms and won’t respond to metronidazole.  \n- Mixed infection: BV plus yeast or trichomonas may need additional/alternate therapy (trichomonas requires oral metronidazole).  \n- Application or adherence issues: missed doses or not completing the full course reduce effectiveness.  \n- Reinfection or persistent risk factors: an untreated sexual partner, new partner, menstrual bleeding, douching, or sex without condoms can allow recurrence.  \n- Biofilm or treatment failure: some BV can be persistent because of biofilm or less responsive bacteria; resistance is uncommon but possible.  \n- Wrong formulation/dose: sometimes oral metronidazole or vaginal clindamycin works better for a particular case.\n\nIf you didn’t get better within 48–72 hours or symptoms returned after finishing treatment, see your clinician for re-evaluation and testing (microscopy, pH, NAAT for trichomonas, GC/CT as indicated). They may switch to oral metronidazole (500 mg twice daily x7 days), use clindamycin, test/treat partners, or investigate other causes. If you want, tell me what symptoms you have and how/when you used the Metrogel and I can suggest next steps.	f	\N
