697	231	11	\N	**B**\n\nExplanation:\nOxytocin is commonly used to induce labor by stimulating uterine contractions. When a patient receives an opioid for pain relief during this process, it can cross the placental barrier and affect the fetus due to its lipophilic nature (fat solubility). One of the primary risks associated with maternal use of intravenous opioids is neonatal respiratory depression. This occurs because opioids bind to mu-receptors in the fetal central nervous system, which can result in diminished responsiveness to carbon dioxide and a consequent decrease in periodic breathing patterns or even apnea (cessation of breathing). Maternal fever is not directly caused by intravenous opioids. Tachycardia refers specifically to an increased heart rate; this symptom would more likely be observed in the mother due to pain, stress, and possibly anxiety rather than as a direct result of maternal use of IV opioids on the fetus. Neonatal acidosis is usually related to hypoxia or hypoperfusion events leading potentially after prolonged periods without adequate oxygen delivery; it doesn't have an immediate connection with intravenous administration of opioids by a mother at term during labor induction, especially in the presence of reassuring fetal heart rates as given. Neonatal arrhythmia is not typically related to maternal use of IV opioids and can be caused by various other factors including intrinsic neonatal conditions or birth stressors; it doesn't directly reflect a common adverse effect from the mother’s intravenous pain medication during labor.\n\nGiven these considerations, E) Neonatal respiratory depression is the most likely answer because of its strong association with maternal use of IV opioids and placental transfer to affect fetal breathing patterns in utero.	f	\N
