806	90	1	\N	## OTC Antifungal (Tinactin) Consideration\n\n**Tinactin (tolnaftate)** is generally a **reasonable option** to try for suspected superficial fungal infections, with some caveats:\n\n### ✅ Generally Safe If:\n- The area is on **skin** (not mucous membranes)\n- It's a typical presentation of **ringworm, athlete's foot, or jock itch**\n- No open wounds or broken skin\n\n### ⚠️ Keep in Mind:\n- Tolnaftate works well for **dermatophytes** (ringworm, tinea)\n- It is **less effective** against *Candida* (yeast) — for that, **clotrimazole or miconazole** (Lotrimin) would be a better choice\n- **Broader coverage** OTC options like **clotrimazole or miconazole** are often preferred as first-line\n\n### 🚩 Stop and See a Doctor If:\n- No improvement after **2 weeks**\n- The area **worsens or spreads**\n- You develop **fever or significant swelling**\n- It's in a **sensitive area** (face, genitals, nails)\n\n**Bottom line:** It's not a bad idea, but **Lotrimin (clotrimazole)** might be a slightly better first choice due to broader antifungal coverage.	t	\N
807	91	1	\N	## Augmentin vs. Amoxicillin\n\n**No, they are not exactly the same**, but they are closely related.\n\n### Key Differences:\n\n| | **Amoxicillin** | **Augmentin** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Components** | Amoxicillin only | Amoxicillin + Clavulanate |\n| **Coverage** | Standard bacterial coverage | Broader coverage |\n\n### What is Clavulanate?\n- Clavulanate (clavulanic acid) is a **beta-lactamase inhibitor**\n- It **blocks enzymes** that some bacteria use to destroy amoxicillin\n- This makes Augmentin effective against **bacteria resistant to amoxicillin alone**\n\n### When Augmentin is Preferred:\n- Sinus infections\n- Ear infections (certain cases)\n- Skin/bite wound infections\n- Infections caused by resistant organisms\n\n### Bottom Line:\n- Augmentin **contains** amoxicillin, but adds clavulanate for broader effectiveness\n- They are **not interchangeable** — Augmentin treats some infections that amoxicillin cannot\n- Augmentin may also cause **more GI side effects** (diarrhea, nausea)\n\nAlways follow your doctor's specific prescription, as the choice between them matters clinically.	t	\N
