1027	56	9	\N	A **white tongue** (leukoplakia-like appearance) can result from several causes, categorized as follows:\n\n### **1. Benign & Common Causes**\n- **Thickened or Coated Tongue (Fur Tongue)**\n  - **Bacterial overgrowth** (e.g., *Candida albicans* infection—**oral thrush**)\n  - **Dry mouth** (xerostomia) from dehydration, mouth breathing, or medications (e.g., antihistamines, antidepressants)\n  - **Poor oral hygiene** (plaque buildup, food debris trapping bacteria)\n  - **Smoking or tobacco use** (irritation + bacterial buildup)\n  - **Alcohol consumption** (dries mouth, alters oral flora)\n\n- **Inflammation or Irritation**\n  - **Glossitis** (inflammation of the tongue, often due to vitamin deficiencies—**B12, iron, folate**)\n  - **Burns or trauma** (hot food/drinks, sharp teeth, dental appliances)\n  - **Allergic reactions** (e.g., to mouthwash, foods, or dental materials)\n\n### **2. Infectious Causes**\n- **Fungal Infections**\n  - **Oral candidiasis (thrush)** – White patches that can be scraped off (common in immunocompromised, diabetics, or infants).\n  - **Geographic tongue (benign migratory glossitis)** – Smooth red patches with white borders (not contagious).\n- **Bacterial Infections**\n  - **Strep throat** (white exudate on tonsils/tongue, often with fever/sore throat).\n  - **Diphtheria** (rare in vaccinated populations; thick gray-white membrane).\n- **Viral Infections**\n  - **Hand, foot, and mouth disease** (white ulcers + rash).\n  - **Herpes simplex (oral herpes)** – Small white/yellow blisters.\n\n### **3. Systemic & Nutritional Deficiencies**\n- **Vitamin Deficiencies**\n  - **B12 (pernicious anemia)** – Smooth red tongue (glossitis).\n  - **Iron (iron-deficiency anemia)** – Tongue may appear pale or smooth.\n  - **Folate (folic acid deficiency)** – Similar to B12 deficiency.\n- **Other Deficiencies**	t	\N
