1066	29	13	\N	Bronchitis does not directly "turn into" pneumonia, as they are distinct infections of different parts of the respiratory tract.\n\n*   **Bronchitis** is an inflammation of the bronchial tubes (airways).\n*   **Pneumonia** is an infection that causes inflammation in the alveoli (air sacs) of the lungs.\n\nHowever, **a severe or untreated case of bronchitis, especially viral bronchitis, can increase the risk of developing pneumonia.** This happens because the inflammation and mucus from bronchitis can impair the lungs' defenses, allowing bacteria or viruses to spread deeper into the lung tissue and cause a secondary pneumonia infection.\n\n**Key point:** While one can lead to the other, they are separate conditions. A worsening illness with high fever, chills, increased shortness of breath, or chest pain should prompt immediate medical evaluation for possible pneumonia.	t	\N
