1030	59	9	\N	**Activated charcoal may help with a stomach bug (gastroenteritis) in specific cases, but it is not a first-line treatment and has limitations:**\n\n### **When It *May* Help:**\n- **If the stomach bug is caused by a toxin-producing bacteria** (e.g., *Staphylococcus aureus*, *Bacillus cereus*, or some *Clostridium* species), charcoal can bind toxins and reduce symptoms.\n- **If taken within 1–2 hours of ingestion** of a known toxin (e.g., poisonous mushrooms, certain drugs, or contaminated food).\n\n### **When It *Does Not* Help:**\n- **Viral gastroenteritis** (e.g., norovirus, rotavirus) – charcoal is ineffective since viruses are not absorbed in the gut.\n- **Bacterial infections** (e.g., *Salmonella*, *E. coli*, *Campylobacter*) – charcoal does not kill bacteria or bind them effectively.\n- **Food poisoning from pre-formed toxins** (e.g., *E. coli* O157:H7, *Vibrio*) – charcoal may help if taken early, but symptoms often appear too late.\n\n### **Risks & Considerations:**\n- **Can worsen diarrhea** by binding nutrients and medications (e.g., antibiotics, antidiarrheals).\n- **May delay proper treatment** (e.g., antibiotics for bacterial infections).\n- **Not recommended for routine use**—symptom management (hydration, rest, antiemetics like ondansetron) is usually better.\n\n### **Bottom Line:**\n- **Only consider charcoal if you suspect toxin ingestion** (e.g., after eating spoiled food or mushrooms).\n- **Do not use for viral/bacterial stomach bugs**—it won’t help and may cause harm.\n- **Seek medical advice** if symptoms persist (fever, bloody stool, dehydration).\n\n**Best approach:** Hydration (oral rehydration salts) + rest. Avoid over-the-counter antidiarrheals if fever or bloody stool is present.	t	\N
