1037	66	9	\N	Yes, **back pain before or during your menstrual cycle (menstruation) is common** and can be linked to several physiological changes, including:\n\n1. **Hormonal Fluctuations** – Progesterone and prostaglandins (hormone-like compounds) rise before menstruation, relaxing pelvic muscles and increasing uterine contractions. This can cause cramping and referred back pain (often in the lower back or sacrum).\n\n2. **Uterine Contraction** – The uterus contracts to shed its lining, which may radiate pain to the lower back.\n\n3. **Pelvic Congestion** – Increased blood flow to the pelvic area before menstruation can cause pressure and discomfort that may refer to the back.\n\n4. **Muscle Tension** – Stress, poor posture, or hormonal changes can lead to muscle tightness in the lower back.\n\n### **When to Seek Medical Attention:**\n- If the pain is **severe, sudden, or accompanied by fever, nausea/vomiting, heavy bleeding, or dizziness** (possible signs of infection, endometriosis, or other conditions).\n- If pain persists **beyond 3 days of menstruation** or worsens with activity.\n\n### **What You Can Do:**\n- **Heat therapy** (heating pad) for muscle relaxation.\n- **Over-the-counter pain relievers** (ibuprofen or naproxen, which also reduce prostaglandins).\n- **Gentle stretching** (e.g., child’s pose, pelvic tilts).\n- **Stay hydrated** and reduce caffeine/salt to minimize bloating.\n\nMost menstrual-related back pain is **not serious**, but if symptoms are unusual or severe, consult a healthcare provider.	t	\N
